window.ACADEMY_DATA = window.ACADEMY_DATA || {quizzes:{}, flashcards:{}, exams:{}};

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"grammar:1": [
{"q":"Mortimer Adler described four levels of reading: elementary, inspectional, analytical, and syntopical. According to the lesson, where do most adults stall — and what does that mean in practice?","choices":["At elementary: they never learned to decode words fluently","At inspectional: they skim for the gist but rarely slow down to analyze","At analytical: they over-analyze and never synthesize","At syntopical: they compare books without reading any closely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most adults read inspectionally — skimming for the gist, which works for triage but not understanding. Grammar trains the next level up, analytical reading: slowing down enough to see exactly what each sentence claims. You cannot evaluate an argument you only skimmed."},
{"q":"A student reads an op-ed, feels strongly it is wrong, but cannot restate the author's premises. Which diagnosis fits the lesson best?","choices":["She read analytically but the argument was invalid","She stalled at the inspectional level — reacting to the gist without parsing the claims","She applied the skeleton method too aggressively","She confused syntopical reading with elementary reading"],"answer":1,"explain":"Strong reaction plus inability to restate premises is the signature of inspectional reading: she absorbed the vibe, not the structure. The lesson's remedy is mechanical parsing — find the main verbs, name what each sentence claims — before judging. Mechanics first, interpretation second."},
{"q":"Why did the trivium place grammar before logic?","choices":["Because Latin was considered more prestigious than reasoning","Because logic evaluates arguments, and you cannot evaluate what you cannot accurately parse","Because children learn languages faster than abstractions","Because rhetoric requires a large vocabulary first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Logic's whole job is judging whether premises support conclusions — but that judgment is worthless if you misread the premises. The lesson calls misreading the most common source of bad reasoning, so parsing skill has to come before evaluation skill."}
],
"grammar:2": [
{"q":"Nearly all of English's abstract vocabulary comes from Latin and Greek. What practical advantage does the lesson claim for learning twenty roots?","choices":["You can speak Latin conversationally within weeks","Thousands of unfamiliar words become decodable without a dictionary","You no longer need to learn grammar rules","English spelling becomes fully regular"],"answer":1,"explain":"Roots are a compression scheme: twenty roots unlock thousands of words because English builds abstract vocabulary by combining them. The lesson's Try section has you decode words like 'circumspect' and 'benevolent' cold — root plus prefix, no dictionary. It will not make spelling regular or teach you to speak Latin."},
{"q":"Using the lesson's roots, decode 'predict'.","choices":["pre (before) + dict (say) — to say beforehand","pre (before) + dict (write) — to write in advance","pred (prey) + ict (strike) — to strike first","pre (before) + dict (rule) — to rule in advance"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pre- means before and dict means say (as in dictate, verdict, contradict), so predict is literally 'to say beforehand.' The distractors mix up dict with neighboring roots — dict is say, not write (scrib) or rule (crat). This is exactly the decoding move the lesson trains."},
{"q":"The lesson's Check asks what 'eudaimonia' breaks into. What is the answer, and why does the lesson bother with a Greek word in a roots lesson?","choices":["Eu (good) + daimon (spirit/fortune): 'flourishing' — it shows roots unlock the central goal-word of Greek ethics","Eu (many) + daimon (demon): 'many demons' — it warns against false friends","Eudai (happy) + monia (mind): 'happy mind' — it introduces psychology terms","Eu (well) + daimonia (fate): 'good fate' — it shows Greek is harder than Latin"],"answer":0,"explain":"Eu- (good) plus daimon (guiding spirit, fortune) gives 'having a good guiding spirit,' translated as flourishing or living well. The lesson uses it to prove the payoff: roots don't just decode trivia, they unlock the load-bearing words of entire philosophies — eudaimonia is the central goal of Greek ethics."}
],
"grammar:3": [
{"q":"What is the 'engine' of a sentence in the skeleton method, and why do you find it first?","choices":["The subject, because it names who the sentence is about","The main verb, because it carries the sentence's claim","The longest modifier, because it holds the most information","The punctuation, because it sets the sentence's boundaries"],"answer":1,"explain":"The main verb is the engine because verbs do the claiming — 'approved,' 'vowed,' 'denied.' Subjects and objects just name the parties. Finding the verb first gives you the claim; everything else is either a party to it or decoration around it."},
{"q":"Apply the skeleton method: 'The mayor, facing mounting pressure from angry residents and local business owners, signed the ordinance late Tuesday.' What is the skeleton?","choices":["The mayor facing pressure signed","Mayor signed ordinance","Residents and owners pressured mayor","The mayor signed late Tuesday"],"answer":1,"explain":"Strip every modifier — 'facing mounting pressure…,' 'angry,' 'local,' 'late Tuesday' — and the machine left standing is subject-verb-object: mayor signed ordinance. Keeping 'late Tuesday' leaves decoration in; the skeleton keeps only what the sentence cannot stand without."},
{"q":"A political speech says: 'My opponent's reckless, job-killing agenda, rejected by economists across the spectrum, will devastate working families.' The skeleton is 'agenda will devastate families.' What does the method reveal?","choices":["That the sentence is grammatically invalid","That 'reckless' and 'job-killing' are doing the persuasive work while the actual claim is a bare prediction","That economists unanimously agree with the speaker","That working families have already been devastated"],"answer":1,"explain":"The skeleton exposes the load-bearing claim — a prediction ('will devastate') — while the adjectives ('reckless, job-killing') and the appeal to economists do the persuading without adding evidence. The lesson's point: fog hides how little is actually being claimed, and the skeleton makes the fog visible."}
],
"grammar:4": [
{"q":"'Puella poetam amat' and 'Poetam puella amat' mean the same thing. Why?","choices":["Latin word order is random and meaning comes from context","The endings mark each word's job — puella is the subject, poetam the object — so position is free","Both sentences use the passive voice","Amat agrees with whichever noun comes first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Latin is inflected: -a marks puella as subject, -am marks poetam as object, so the roles are assigned by endings, not position. Word order in Latin is free for emphasis rather than required for meaning — the opposite of English, where order does the job endings do."},
{"q":"Apply the endings: in 'Poetam puella amat,' who loves whom?","choices":["The poet loves the girl","The girl loves the poet","They love each other","The sentence is ungrammatical"],"answer":1,"explain":"Puella (nominative, -a) is the subject — the girl does the loving. Poetam (accusative, -am) is the object — the poet is loved. Amat means 'he/she loves,' with the pronoun carried by the -t ending. Order changed, meaning didn't — exactly the lesson's point."},
{"q":"The -t in 'amat' carries the pronoun ('he/she loves') without any separate word for 'he' or 'she.' What does this show about Latin verbs?","choices":["Latin has no pronouns at all","Verb endings encode the subject, so explicit pronouns are usually unnecessary","All Latin verbs are irregular","Latin verbs agree with the object, not the subject"],"answer":1,"explain":"Amo = I love, amas = you love, amat = he/she loves: the ending does the pronoun's work. That is why the lesson says the ending 'carries the pronoun.' Latin does have pronouns, but they are used for emphasis — the verb already knows who is acting."}
],
"grammar:5": [
{"q":"'Carpe diem' is usually translated 'seize the day,' but the lesson notes that 'carpe' literally means 'pluck,' as in fruit. How does that change the motto's meaning?","choices":["It doesn't — translation is always approximate anyway","It shifts the image from grabbing to harvesting: the day is ripe and must be gathered at the right moment, not seized by force","It proves Horace was writing about farming, not life","It makes the phrase a warning against ambition"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plucking is skilled, timely work — you harvest fruit when it is ripe, neither early nor late. 'Seize' suggests force; 'pluck' suggests attention and timing. The lesson includes the literal meaning precisely because the metaphor changes: the day isn't an enemy to grab but a crop to gather well."},
{"q":"A Stoic facing an unexpected career setback says 'amor fati.' According to the lesson, what are they practicing?","choices":["Passive resignation — accepting that nothing can be done","Actively affirming the setback as the material they're given to work with, not merely enduring it","Denial — pretending the setback didn't happen","A Roman good-luck ritual for job interviews"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Love of one's fate' goes past acceptance: instead of merely tolerating what you can't control, you affirm it as your starting material. The lesson's Check answer is explicit — the Stoic use is for events outside your control, turned from obstacles into the stuff you work with."},
{"q":"Match the phrase to its source: 'Veni, vidi, vici.'","choices":["Horace, in a poem about the countryside","Caesar — 'I came, I saw, I conquered'","Nietzsche, reviving a Stoic motto","Augustus, as his personal motto"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson attributes 'Veni, vidi, vici' to Caesar. The distractors are the lesson's other attributions, deliberately shuffled: Horace wrote 'carpe diem,' Nietzsche revived 'amor fati,' and Augustus's motto was 'festina lente' (from the capstone). Keeping sources straight is part of the grammar habit."}
],
"grammar:6": [
{"q":"'Festina lente' was Augustus's motto. Translate it and explain the paradox it resolves.","choices":["'Hurry slowly' — it resolves haste versus care: move fast but without rashness","'Festivals end' — a reminder that celebrations are brief","'Stand firm lightly' — advice for soldiers in formation","'Hasten the law' — a command to judges"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Make haste slowly' holds two opposed virtues together: speed matters, but rash speed destroys. Augustus — who built an empire — used it to mean deliberate urgency: act quickly, but never so quickly that you stop thinking. The paradox is the point, not a contradiction."},
{"q":"Which capstone phrase means 'Fortune favors the bold,' and which word carries 'the bold'?","choices":["Dum spiro spero — spiro","Audentes fortuna iuvat — audentes","Festina lente — lente","Per aspera ad astra — aspera"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Audentes fortuna iuvat': audentes means 'the daring ones' (the bold), fortuna is fortune, iuvat is 'helps, favors' — so 'fortune favors the bold.' Dum spiro spero is 'while I breathe, I hope,' and festina lente is 'make haste slowly.' The capstone drills exactly this: tell the mottos apart by their load-bearing words."},
{"q":"The capstone pairs two tasks: parsing English prose into skeletons and translating Latin mottos. What single skill do both tasks train?","choices":["Memorization of vocabulary lists","Extracting exactly what a sentence claims before interpreting it","Speed reading for examinations","Creative writing in two languages"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both tasks are extraction exercises: the skeleton strips English fog to the bare claim, and translation forces you to pin down what each Latin word contributes before rendering the sense. The course's thesis is that interpretation must wait on accurate extraction — mechanics first, interpretation second — and the capstone makes you do both mechanics."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"logic:1": [
{"q":"'All cats are mammals. Whiskers is a cat. Therefore Whiskers is a mammal.' The lesson calls this both valid and sound. What would make it valid but NOT sound?","choices":["If Whiskers turned out to be a dog — the structure holds but a premise is false","If the conclusion were reworded more politely","If it had four premises instead of two","Validity and soundness are the same thing, so this is impossible"],"answer":0,"explain":"Validity is structure: if the premises were true, the conclusion must be true — and that hypothetical guarantee survives regardless of reality. Soundness adds the second question: are the premises actually true? If 'Whiskers is a cat' is false, the machine runs perfectly on bad fuel — valid, unsound, conclusion unsupported."},
{"q":"Consider: 'All birds can fly. Penguins are birds. Therefore penguins can fly.' Valid? Sound?","choices":["Invalid and unsound — the conclusion is false","Valid but unsound — the form guarantees the conclusion only if the premises hold, and the first premise is false","Sound but invalid — the premises are true","Neither valid nor sound — penguins aren't birds"],"answer":1,"explain":"The form is the same guaranteed machine as the cats example — that is validity, and it doesn't care that penguins exist. But 'all birds can fly' is false, so the argument is unsound. The lesson's warning: a valid argument with bad premises can deliver a false conclusion with total confidence."},
{"q":"An argument is invalid, yet its conclusion happens to be true. What has the arguer actually established?","choices":["Soundness, because the conclusion is true","Nothing — a true conclusion from broken structure is a lucky guess, not knowledge","Validity, retroactively","That the premises must be true"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson is blunt: only soundness gives you knowledge. An invalid argument is a broken machine — if it happens to output a true sentence, that is luck, not reasoning. Truth of the conclusion never repairs the structure; you still don't know it by that argument."}
],
"logic:2": [
{"q":"'If the alarm is armed, the light blinks. The light is blinking. So the alarm is armed.' What is wrong with this argument?","choices":["Nothing — it is modus ponens","It affirms the consequent: the light could blink for other reasons, so the conclusion doesn't follow","It denies the antecedent","It is sound but invalid"],"answer":1,"explain":"This is affirming the consequent: P implies Q, Q, therefore P. The lesson's rain example makes the flaw vivid — wet ground doesn't prove rain, because sprinklers exist. Here, a faulty wire or a test mode could blink the light. The conclusion might be true, but this argument doesn't establish it."},
{"q":"'If the alarm is armed, the light blinks. The light is not blinking. So the alarm is not armed.' Which valid form is this?","choices":["Affirming the consequent","Denying the antecedent","Modus tollens","Modus ponens"],"answer":2,"explain":"Modus tollens: if P then Q; not-Q; therefore not-P. If the alarm were armed, the light would have to blink — it doesn't, so the alarm isn't armed. Contrast with denying the antecedent (not-P, therefore not-Q), which is invalid: the light could blink while unarmed, but it can't stay dark while armed."},
{"q":"'If he's guilty he'll flee. He didn't flee. So he's innocent.' The lesson's Check asked about the fleeing version. How does this version differ logically?","choices":["It is the same fallacy with the facts reversed","It uses the valid form modus tollens — though the first premise ('guilty people flee') may still be false","It is sound because innocent people don't flee","It begs the question"],"answer":1,"explain":"Form and truth are separate questions. Not-fleeing combined with 'guilty implies flees' is modus tollens, a valid form — the machine works. But validity isn't soundness: the premise 'if guilty then flees' is shaky (guilty people often stay), so the argument is valid yet possibly unsound. The lesson's Check version failed at the form level; this one passes form and fails, if anywhere, at the premises."}
],
"logic:3": [
{"q":"'Don't listen to her budget proposal — she failed math in college.' Which fallacy, and why does it fail?","choices":["Strawman — it restates her proposal weakly","Ad hominem — it attacks the proposer instead of engaging the proposal's numbers","Tu quoque — it accuses her of hypocrisy","Genetic fallacy — it judges the proposal by its origin"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ad hominem: the attack is on the arguer, not the argument. Her college transcript is irrelevant to whether the budget numbers add up — the lesson's version ('convicted fraudster') makes the same point: even a true personal charge doesn't touch the claim. Evaluate the proposal, not the proposer."},
{"q":"'You say we should cut emissions, yet you flew here on a private jet.' Which fallacy is this, and what is the key point about it?","choices":["Ad hominem — it insults the speaker","Tu quoque ('you too') — hypocrisy doesn't refute the claim; the emissions argument stands or falls on its own","Strawman — it misstates the emissions position","Appeal to authority — it cites jet owners as experts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tu quoque points at inconsistency between the speaker's life and their claim. The lesson's key point: hypocrisy is a character charge, not a refutation — the claim about emissions is either true or false regardless of the speaker's travel. Note the difference from ad hominem: this one grants the argument exists and attacks via the speaker's conduct."},
{"q":"A famous actor endorses a cryptocurrency as a safe investment. A fan invests because 'he's so successful.' Which fallacy, and when WOULD an authority appeal be legitimate?","choices":["Ad hominem; never — authorities are always fallacious","Illegitimate appeal to authority — acting success is outside financial expertise; it would be legitimate only from relevant-field experts with genuine consensus, on claims within their competence","Genetic fallacy; when the authority is wealthy","Strawman; when the claim is about acting"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson distinguishes the fallacy from the legitimate move: citing an expert outside their field (or a vague 'studies show') is the fallacy. 'Experts agree' is fine when the experts are in the relevant field, there is real consensus, and the claim is within their competence — an actor's success meets none of those conditions for investment advice."}
],
"logic:4": [
{"q":"'We either ban phones in schools entirely or watch a generation rot.' Which fallacy?","choices":["False dilemma — it presents two options where more exist","Slippery slope — it predicts a chain of disasters","Post hoc — it confuses timing with cause","Cherry-picking — it selects favorable data"],"answer":0,"explain":"False dilemma: the arguer welds the choice to two extremes — total ban or civilizational rot — hiding the middle: restricted use, lockable pouches, classroom-only bans, digital-literacy curricula. The lesson's example ('ban it or society collapses') is the same move. Always ask: what options were deleted?"},
{"q":"'Ever since the new coach arrived, test scores have risen — so the coach caused the improvement.' Which fallacy, and what is the core confusion?","choices":["Post hoc ergo propter hoc — sequence is not causation; the rise may have other causes","Hasty generalization — one coach is too small a sample","Begging the question — the conclusion is assumed","False dilemma — coaching versus no coaching"],"answer":0,"explain":"'After this, therefore because of this.' Timing alone never establishes cause — scores might reflect a new curriculum, an easier test, or a trend that predates the coach. The lesson's crime-and-mayor Check makes the same demand: before believing the implied claim, check confounds, longer trends, and base rates."},
{"q":"'Three of my friends felt ill after the new vaccine, so it must be dangerous.' Which fallacy, and what would strengthen the reasoning into induction?","choices":["Slippery slope; a longer chain of consequences","Hasty generalization — three anecdotes are not a trend; it would need a large, varied, properly collected sample","Begging the question; a clearer definition of 'dangerous'","Genetic fallacy; knowing who made the vaccine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three anecdotes treated as a trend is hasty generalization. The lesson's standards for induction say generalization strength depends on sample size, variety, and method — three self-selected friends fail all three. Proper induction would need controlled, representative data, not stories."}
],
"logic:5": [
{"q":"A detective notes the lawn is wet, the sky is clear, and the sprinkler is running, and concludes the sprinkler caused it. Which mode of reasoning is this?","choices":["Deduction — the conclusion follows with certainty","Induction — generalizing from many wet lawns","Abduction — inference to the best explanation of the evidence","Circular reasoning — assuming what must be proved"],"answer":2,"explain":"Abduction: the detective isn't deducing (no guaranteed premises) or generalizing from cases — she's picking the explanation that best fits all the evidence. The lesson notes this is how diagnosis, detective work, and much of science actually run: best current explanation, held revisably."},
{"q":"'Every swan I have seen is white, so all swans are white.' This is induction. What is its permanent vulnerability?","choices":["It uses too few premises","It is never certain — one black swan breaks it","It confuses cause and effect","It attacks the arguer instead of the argument"],"answer":1,"explain":"Induction projects from observed cases to a general rule, and no number of white swans can close the logical gap — a single black swan falsifies the universal claim. That is why the lesson says induction is powerful but never certain, with strength depending on sample size, variety, and method rather than on proof."},
{"q":"Why is 'inference to the best explanation' not just guessing?","choices":["Because it always produces the true answer","Because it is constrained: it must account for all the evidence, survive rival explanations, and stay revisable","Because experts do it","Because it uses more data than deduction"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's Check answer lists three constraints that separate abduction from guessing: fit all the evidence (not just the convenient bits), beat the rival explanations (Epictetus's discipline of assent: always ask what else could explain the impression), and remain revisable when new evidence arrives. Guessing skips all three."}
],
"logic:6": [
{"q":"The capstone's first step is: state the conclusion in your own words. Why is this step non-negotiable?","choices":["It makes the teardown longer and more impressive","It prevents strawmanning — you cannot fairly evaluate an argument you have restated in a weaker form","It is required by copyright law","It lets you skip reading the whole piece"],"answer":1,"explain":"Restating in your own words forces comprehension and blocks the strawman reflex — defeating a weakened paraphrase proves nothing about the actual claim. The lesson explicitly warns 'no strawmanning' at this step: charity first, then criticism. If you can't state it fairly, you haven't understood it."},
{"q":"A viral post says: 'A senator said the new policy failed.' Under the capstone's accuracy standard, what does this verify?","choices":["That the policy failed","Only that the senator made the claim — not that the claim is true","That the senator is an expert on the policy","Nothing at all — senators never count as sources"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's standard is exact: 'accurate means the claim is true as stated — not merely that someone said it.' 'X said Y' verifies the saying, never the Y. The teardown must then check the claim itself: premises marked verified, unverified, or false on their own evidence."},
{"q":"Your teardown finds the argument's structure is valid, but every premise is marked 'unverified.' What is the correct verdict?","choices":["The argument is sound — validity is enough","The conclusion is proven true","The argument establishes nothing yet — validity without true premises is not soundness, so the conclusion is unsupported","The argument commits the strawman fallacy"],"answer":2,"explain":"This is the two-questions discipline from lesson 1 applied at the end: question 1 (valid?) passed, question 2 (sound? are the premises true?) can't be answered yes. A valid machine running on unverified fuel yields no knowledge. The honest verdict: interesting structure, unsupported conclusion — go verify the premises."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"rhetoric:1": [
{"q":"Of the five canons, three are about thinking and two about performing. Which grouping is correct?","choices":["Thinking: invention, arrangement, style. Performing: memory, delivery","Thinking: memory, delivery, style. Performing: invention, arrangement","Thinking: invention, delivery. Performing: arrangement, style, memory","All five are about performing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Invention (finding arguments), arrangement (ordering them), and style (choosing words) are thinking work — they happen before anyone hears you. Memory and delivery are performance. The lesson's complaint is that modern speaking advice inverts the ratio: all delivery polish, no invention — 'confident speakers with nothing to say.'"},
{"q":"A speaker is polished, confident, and utterly empty — the audience remembers the performance and nothing else. Which canon did they skip, and why does it matter most?","choices":["Delivery — they needed more gestures","Invention — they never found anything worth saying; polish can't substitute for arguments","Memory — they should have memorized more","Arrangement — the speech was too short"],"answer":1,"explain":"Invention is finding what to say — the topics, the evidence, the actual arguments. The lesson's diagnosis of modern speaking culture is exactly this speaker: delivery-obsessed, invention-starved. No amount of voice coaching creates a case; it only amplifies the emptiness."},
{"q":"Why is rhetoric not manipulation, according to the lesson?","choices":["Because audiences enjoy being manipulated","Because rhetoric is the art of effective communication; logic constrains what you may honestly claim, and misuse is the speaker's ethical failure, not the art's","Because manipulation requires no skill","Because the ancients never lied"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson draws the line at honesty of content: logic sets what you may truthfully claim, rhetoric handles how true claims are best conveyed. Using these tools to mislead is like using a knife as a weapon — a failure of the user's ethics, not of the tool. The art itself is neutral; the speaker isn't."}
],
"rhetoric:2": [
{"q":"Two colleagues argue about remote work. One cites productivity data showing output rose; the other replies, 'But is it really working if nobody's in the office?' What has happened, in stasis terms?","choices":["They agree on policy but differ on conjecture","They're at different stasis points — consequences versus definition — so their arguments pass each other","They're both at the quality stasis","Stasis theory doesn't apply to workplace disputes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first argues consequences (output rose — a policy/quality consideration); the second disputes the definition of 'working.' The lesson's rule: most doomed arguments are people answering different stasis questions. Until they agree on which question they're answering — what counts as 'working'? — no evidence can land."},
{"q":"'Half of all disputes are definition disputes.' Which topic (topos) resolves them, and what does it ask?","choices":["Comparison — what is this like?","Definition — what exactly are we talking about?","Testimony — who has seen this?","Consequences — what follows if we act?"],"answer":1,"explain":"The definition topic asks the most basic question: what exactly are we talking about? The lesson claims half of all disputes dissolve or clarify here — people argue past each other using the same word for different things. Pin the meaning first; argue second."},
{"q":"You're arguing for a policy and keep losing. Which topic asks 'If we act, what follows? If we don't?' — and why is it powerful?","choices":["Testimony — because records persuade","Consequences — because it surfaces arguments about outcomes you hadn't considered, for and against","Comparison — because analogies win debates","Definition — because terms must be clear"],"answer":1,"explain":"The consequences topic forces you to trace effects of acting and of not acting — and the lesson's Try promises this surfaces your two strongest unthought-of arguments. It's powerful because most arguers only trace one branch (usually the scary one); the topic demands both."}
],
"rhetoric:3": [
{"q":"A pitch opens: 'So, um, thanks for having me — I'll just, uh, dive right in, I guess.' What went wrong in the exordium?","choices":["Nothing — humility builds goodwill","Throat-clearing replaced the opening's real jobs: winning attention and goodwill, starting with the stakes","The speaker should have skipped to the peroratio","Exordiums are only for written essays"],"answer":1,"explain":"The exordium must win attention and goodwill, and the lesson's rule is 'start with the stakes, not throat-clearing.' Filler apologies spend the audience's freshest attention on nothing. Goodwill comes from respecting their time — which means opening like the matter is urgent, because you've asked for their minutes."},
{"q":"According to the lesson, where in the six-part speech is credibility won — and why there?","choices":["In the exordium — first impressions decide everything","In the refutatio — answering objections before the audience raises them shows fairness and defuses resistance","In the peroratio — the close is what they remember","In the narratio — facts alone persuade"],"answer":1,"explain":"The refutatio is where you steelman the other side and answer objections the audience is already thinking. Doing it before they raise them signals confidence and fairness — the lesson says 'this is where credibility is won.' A speaker who hides from objections looks afraid of them; one who meets them looks trustworthy."},
{"q":"Why does the confirmatio order arguments weakest-to-strongest instead of leading with the best?","choices":["Weak arguments need the most help","Recency and momentum: the strongest point carries into the refutatio and peroratio, while leading with it spends the best ammunition before the audience is warmed up","The audience forgets the middle anyway","Classical audiences preferred suspense"],"answer":1,"explain":"Endings dominate memory and momentum: your strongest argument should be the last thing heard before you turn to objections and the close. Leading with it wastes peak force on a cold audience and leaves the close running on fumes. Arrangement is applied psychology — order is force."}
],
"rhetoric:4": [
{"q":"The lesson's example of anaphora is 'we shall fight… we shall fight…' What defines anaphora?","choices":["Three parallel phrases of equal length","Repetition of a word or phrase at the start of successive clauses","Contrasting 'not X but Y' in one sentence","Deleting all adjectives for plainness"],"answer":1,"explain":"Anaphora is repetition at the beginning — carrying the same opening back each time. Churchill's 'we shall fight' hammers resolve by refusing to vary the opening. Contrast tricolon (three parallel members, any position) and antithesis ('not X but Y'). The lesson's caution stands: one such device earns its keep; a speech stuffed with them is decoration."},
{"q":"'It is imperative that we leverage our core competencies to operationalize a paradigm shift in customer-facing deliverables.' Which plain-style rules does this violate?","choices":["None — this is exemplary plain style","It throat-clears ('it is imperative that') and hides behind abstractions instead of concrete words","It uses active voice too aggressively","Its sentences are too short for punch"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two violations from the lesson's list: throat-clearing ('it is imperative that' — delete) and abstract jargon ('leverage core competencies,' 'operationalize a paradigm shift') where concrete words belong. The lesson's own Try makes you rewrite this — and then rewrite it grand, to feel which one you'd actually trust."},
{"q":"A manager announces a routine schedule change in full grand style — soaring cadences, historic gravity. What is the likely effect, per the lesson?","choices":["The team is moved to costly action","It sounds pompous and destroys trust — the grand style is for eulogies, crises, and calls to sacrifice, not routine matters","The message becomes clearer","The plain style is always wrong for managers"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson reserves the grand style for moments that must move people to costly action — eulogies, crisis speeches, calls to sacrifice. Spending it on a schedule change is stylistic inflation: the audience hears pomp where the occasion warrants plainness, and the mismatch reads as untrustworthy. Style must fit the stakes."}
],
"rhetoric:5": [
{"q":"A charity ad shows a suffering child, swelling music — and no information about where donations go. Diagnose it with the lesson's ethical test.","choices":["Ethical persuasion — pathos always justifies itself","Manipulation — pathos as a substitute for logos; the appeal would not survive the audience learning the full facts","Logos-heavy — the data speaks for itself","Ethos-building — the charity looks honest"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's test: pathos in service of a true logos is persuasion; pathos as a substitute for logos is manipulation — would the emotional appeal survive full facts? Here emotion replaces the missing accountability (where does money go?), so the ad fails the test. A version with the same story plus real financials would pass it."},
{"q":"Aristotle lists logos first, but the lesson says audiences process the appeals in reverse order of prestige: ethos, then pathos, then logos. Why does logos come last in practice?","choices":["Because logic is unimportant in persuasion","Because audiences decide whether to trust the speaker and whether to care before they grant the argument itself a hearing","Because ancient audiences were uneducated","Because pathos is a type of logos"],"answer":1,"explain":"'Should I trust this person?' (ethos, decided in the first minute) and 'Do I care?' (pathos) are gatekeepers — no one evaluates reasoning from a speaker they've dismissed or about a subject that leaves them cold. Logos only gets its hearing after the other two open the door. That is why the lesson orders them ethos, pathos, logos 'in that order.'"},
{"q":"You're addressing a hostile, skeptical room. What is the fastest ethos move, and why does it work?","choices":["Open with your credentials and awards","Concede something real — admit a limit, grant their strongest point, or disclose an interest — because costly honesty is the most credible signal there is","Flatter the audience's intelligence","Speak louder to project confidence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's Check answer: costly honesty is the most credible signal. Conceding something real — especially granting the opponent's strongest point — is expensive for the speaker, which is exactly why the audience believes it: nobody pays that cost for a trick. Credentials can be bought; a voluntary concession can't be faked cheaply."}
],
"rhetoric:6": [
{"q":"Why does the method of loci outperform rote repetition?","choices":["Because repetition damages memory","Because it piggybacks on the brain's ancient spatial-navigation system — stronger than the verbal rehearsal loop — adding vivid, distinctive, ordered retrieval cues","Because Roman orators had better brains","Because it eliminates the need to understand the material"],"answer":1,"explain":"Human memory is spatial first, verbal second: evolution built powerful place-memory long before language. The loci method exploits it — each point gets a vivid location on a familiar route, giving you ordered, distinctive cues instead of one fragile verbal chain. Modern memory champions still use exactly this; rote repetition leans on the weaker system."},
{"q":"A speaker rushes through her key points with no pauses, and the audience retains nothing. Which delivery rules from the lesson address this?","choices":["Gesture from the wrist and speak faster to hold attention","Speak slightly slower than feels natural, and pause after important sentences — silence is punctuation","Plant your feet and never move","Eliminate all nerves before speaking"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two rules: slow down past what feels natural (adrenaline speeds you up; the audience needs processing time) and let silence punctuate — a pause after a key sentence marks it as important and gives it room to land. The lesson treats pauses as punctuation, not dead air: they tell the audience where the weight is."},
{"q":"'Nerves are fuel — the goal isn't calm, it's controlled energy.' What does this imply for how you prepare?","choices":["Try to eliminate nervousness completely before speaking","Channel the arousal into the performance rather than fighting it; preparation should aim at control, not tranquility","Avoid preparation, since it increases nerves","Drink something calming right before"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson reframes nerves as energy to direct, not a defect to cure — attempting total calm usually produces flat delivery or panicked suppression. Preparation (loci-memorized structure, rehearsed pauses, planted feet) gives the energy rails to run on. Controlled energy reads as conviction; forced calm reads as lifeless."}
],
"rhetoric:7": [
{"q":"The capstone allows exactly one tricolon or anaphora in the whole speech. Why the strict limit?","choices":["Devices are forbidden in classical rhetoric","A speech full of devices is a fireworks show, not an argument — restraint keeps each device meaningful","Audiences can't understand parallel structure","The capstone is only about memory"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's style chapter warns that schemes 'earn their keep' only when scarce: one tricolon lands like a hammer; five in a row become wallpaper the audience stops hearing. Restraint is what separates a device used for force from decoration used for show — and the capstone enforces the discipline physically."},
{"q":"Why must the capstone's refutatio answer your STRONGEST objection honestly, rather than a weak one?","choices":["Weak objections don't exist","Credibility is won in the refutatio — meeting the strongest objection head-on signals confidence and fairness, while dodging it signals fear","The audience enjoys long speeches","Strong objections are easier to answer"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson says the refutatio is 'where credibility is won': answering objections before the audience raises them. Tackling the strongest one honestly proves you're not afraid of your own case's weak points — the costly-honesty ethos move. Swatting a straw objection instead tells a sharp audience exactly what you're hiding from."},
{"q":"'Ask your listener what they remember tomorrow. What survives 24 hours is what worked.' What standard of success does this set?","choices":["Success is measured by applause in the moment","Success is lasting effect on the listener — persuasion, not performance; the speech worked only if it stuck","Success means perfect memorization","Success is delivering exactly three minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The capstone's final question — 'did you persuade, or merely perform?' — draws the line between impressing a room and changing a mind. Applause measures the moment; next-day recall measures the residue. It's the rhetoric course's thesis in one test: the art serves the audience's lasting understanding, not the speaker's fleeting glory."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.flashcards, {
"grammar": [
{"front":"Analytical reading (Adler)","back":"Adler's level above inspectional: slowing down to see exactly what each sentence claims. Grammar trains this — most adults stall at inspectional (skimming for gist). Hook: grammar is X-ray vision for sentences."},
{"front":"Parsing","back":"The ancients' core exercise: taking sentences apart word by word and naming each word's job. Feels mechanical — that is the point. Mechanics first, interpretation second."},
{"front":"The skeleton method","back":"1) Find the main verb (the engine). 2) Who/what before it = subject. 3) Whom/what after it = object. 4) Cross out modifiers; the skeleton must still stand. Example: 'The committee, after three hours of bitter debate, approved the budget' → committee approved budget."},
{"front":"Inflection (Latin)","back":"Endings that mark a word's job: puella (subject), puellam (object), puellae (of/to the girl). Because endings assign roles, Latin word order is free — English uses order where Latin uses endings."},
{"front":"dict (Latin root)","back":"'Say.' Dictate, verdict, contradict, predict (= pre + dict, 'say beforehand'). Anchor: a verdict is the jury's 'say.'"},
{"front":"spec (Latin root)","back":"'Look.' Inspect, spectacle, retrospect, circumspect (= circum + spec, 'looking around' → cautious). Anchor: spectacles are for looking."},
{"front":"Carpe diem","back":"Horace. Literally 'pluck the day' — carpe means pluck, as in fruit. Not seizing by force but harvesting at the right moment: the day is ripe, gather it with timing and skill."},
{"front":"Memento mori","back":"'Remember you must die.' Memento = remember! (command), mori = to die. Not morbidity — a reminder of limits that sharpens how you spend the day."}
],
"logic": [
{"front":"Validity","back":"If the premises were true, would the conclusion HAVE to be true? Purely about structure, not reality. A valid argument can still have a false conclusion (bad premises)."},
{"front":"Soundness","back":"Valid AND the premises are actually true. Only soundness gives you knowledge. Soundness is always hostage to the real world."},
{"front":"Modus ponens","back":"If P then Q; P; therefore Q. Valid. Example: if it rains the ground gets wet; it rains; so the ground gets wet."},
{"front":"Modus tollens","back":"If P then Q; not Q; therefore not P. Valid. Example: if the policy worked prices would fall; prices didn't fall; so the policy didn't work."},
{"front":"Affirming the consequent","back":"If P then Q; Q; therefore P. INVALID — sprinklers exist (wet ground doesn't prove rain). The lesson's classic trap."},
{"front":"Ad hominem","back":"Attacking the arguer instead of the argument: 'You can't trust his tax plan — he's a convicted fraudster.' Maybe true of him; irrelevant to whether the plan works."},
{"front":"Post hoc ergo propter hoc","back":"'After this, therefore because of this.' Sequence isn't causation. Antidote: check confounds, longer trends, base rates — the crime-and-mayor headline drill."},
{"front":"Abduction","back":"Inference to the best explanation (wet lawn + clear sky + running sprinkler → the sprinkler did it). Not guessing: it must fit ALL the evidence, survive rival explanations, and stay revisable."}
],
"rhetoric": [
{"front":"The five canons","back":"Invention (find arguments), Arrangement (order them), Style (choose words), Memory (internalize), Delivery (voice/gesture). Three are thinking, two are performing — modern advice obsesses over delivery and starves invention."},
{"front":"Stasis theory","back":"Before arguing, name the question: Conjecture (did it happen?), Definition (what do we call it?), Quality (was it justified?), Policy (what should we do?). Most doomed arguments = people answering different stasis questions past each other."},
{"front":"Exordium","back":"The opening: win attention and goodwill. Start with the stakes, not throat-clearing. The audience's freshest attention is spent here — don't waste it on filler."},
{"front":"Refutatio","back":"Answer the objections BEFORE the audience raises them. This is where credibility is won — it signals confidence and fairness. Tackle the strongest objection honestly."},
{"front":"Tricolon","back":"Three parallel phrases: 'life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.' Use sparingly — a speech full of devices is a fireworks show, not an argument."},
{"front":"Anaphora","back":"Repetition at the start of successive clauses: 'we shall fight… we shall fight…' Hammers resolve by refusing to vary the opening. One earns its keep; five become wallpaper."},
{"front":"Ethos → pathos → logos","back":"The audience's actual order: trust the speaker (ethos, decided in minute one), care about the subject (pathos), THEN hear the argument (logos). Lead with charts to a cold room and the logos never gets its hearing."},
{"front":"Method of loci","back":"Place each point at a location along a familiar route, in order; mentally walk the route to recall. Works because memory is spatial first, verbal second — memory champions still use it."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.exams, {
"grammar": [
{"q":"Adler's four levels are elementary, inspectional, analytical, syntopical. Where do most adults stall, and what is the practical symptom?","choices":["Elementary — they can't decode words","Inspectional — they skim for the gist and rarely slow down to analyze claims","Analytical — they parse but can't synthesize","Syntopical — they compare without reading"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's diagnosis: most adults read well enough to skim but stop at the gist — inspectional reading. The symptom is confident opinions about texts they couldn't accurately summarize. Grammar is prescribed as the cure because it forces the analytical level: claim-by-claim slowdown."},
{"q":"Skeleton drill: 'The senator, pressed by reporters after the leaked memo surfaced, denied any knowledge of the payments.' What is the skeleton — and what does it usefully expose?","choices":["Senator denied knowledge — it exposes that the claim is a bare denial, with the pressure and the memo as surrounding fog","Reporters pressed senator — the reporters are the real subject","The memo surfaced — the memo is the engine","Senator denied reporters — the object is the press"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strip 'pressed by reporters,' 'after the leaked memo surfaced,' 'any,' 'of the payments' — the machine is subject-verb-object: senator denied knowledge. The method's payoff: you see the denial is total ('any knowledge') while the interesting material (the memo, the pressure) sits in the modifiers, unclaimed and unexamined."},
{"q":"In 'Poetam puella amat,' which word is the object, and how do you know?","choices":["Puella — it comes second","Poetam — the -am ending marks the accusative (object), regardless of position","Amat — verbs take the object role in Latin","Cannot be determined without more context"],"answer":1,"explain":"Poetam carries the -am accusative ending: the poet is the one loved. Puella's -a is nominative: the girl loves. Position is irrelevant in Latin — the lesson's whole point with the reversed word order. In English you'd need the order; in Latin the endings do the work."},
{"q":"'Eudaimonia' breaks into eu- (good) + daimon (spirit/fortune): 'flourishing.' What does the lesson's use of this word show about learning roots?","choices":["That roots help you count how often words appear in texts","That roots let you decode the load-bearing words entire philosophies turn on, instead of memorizing them as blobs","That roots are mainly about pronunciation","That roots track which author coined a word"],"answer":1,"explain":"Greek ethics is organized around achieving eudaimonia — decode the word and you grasp the target of the whole tradition. The lesson's point: roots aren't trivia; they're keys to the words entire philosophies turn on. Decode the root, and the concept arrives with its etymology attached."},
{"q":"'Manuscript' and 'photograph' share an idea across two languages. What is it?","choices":["Both mean 'light-writing'","Both contain a root meaning write/draw — scrib (Latin) in manuscript, graph (Greek) in photograph","Both are Latin words","They share no root; it's coincidence"],"answer":1,"explain":"Manu-script: hand + write (Latin scrib). Photo-graph: light + write/draw (Greek graph). Two languages, one concept — the lesson's table lists both roots with the same gloss. Seeing the shared idea across the Latin/Greek divide is exactly the decoding fluency the lesson builds."},
{"q":"'Festina lente' — translate it, name whose motto it was, and state the tension it holds.","choices":["'Make haste slowly' — Augustus — the tension between speed and care: act urgently but never rashly","'Festivals end' — Horace — the tension between joy and sorrow","'Hasten slowly' — Caesar — the tension between war and peace","'Stand firm' — Seneca — the tension between courage and fear"],"answer":0,"explain":"Augustus's motto: festina (hasten!) + lente (slowly) — 'make haste slowly.' The tension is deliberate urgency: an empire-builder commanding speed while forbidding rashness. The capstone groups it with 'dum spiro spero' and 'audentes fortuna iuvat' precisely so you learn to distinguish mottos by their load-bearing words."},
{"q":"Two mottos for two moments: you suffer an unjust setback; your friend wins big and grows arrogant. Which motto fits each, and why?","choices":["Amor fati for the setback (affirm fate as material to work with); memento mori for the friend (remember limits amid success)","Memento mori for the setback; amor fati for the friend","Carpe diem for both — seize everything","E pluribus unum for both — unity heals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Amor fati is the lesson's tool for events outside your control — the setback becomes material, not just misfortune. Memento mori ('remember you must die') is the check on success: limits remembered in triumph prevent arrogance. The lesson teaches both as practices, not decorations — each answers a specific human moment."},
{"q":"The capstone pairs parsing English prose with translating Latin mottos. What single discipline do both train, in the course's own terms?","choices":["Memorization — both are vocabulary drills","Mechanics before interpretation: extracting exactly what a sentence claims before judging or rendering it","Speed — both are timed exercises","Creativity — both invite free paraphrase"],"answer":1,"explain":"The course's thesis, stated in lesson 1: 'Mechanics first, interpretation second.' The skeleton extracts the English claim; translation pins each Latin word's contribution before rendering sense. Both forbid the besetting sin — reacting to the gist — and enforce the same discipline: know exactly what was said before deciding what it means."},
{"q":"Decode 'circumspect' with the lesson's roots.","choices":["Circum (around) + spec (look) — 'looking around,' hence cautious and careful","Circu (circle) + spect (ghost) — 'seeing ghosts'","Cir (sir) + cumspect (respect) — 'respectful sir'","Circum (around) + spect (hope) — 'hopeful in all directions'"],"answer":0,"explain":"Circum- = around, spec = look (inspect, spectacle, retrospect): literally 'looking around.' Someone who looks around before acting is cautious — the meaning falls out of the parts. The lesson's Try assigns this as a cold decode; it's the payoff of the table: the word was never memorized, yet it's now transparent."},
{"q":"'The report suggests the policy may have contributed to the decline.' Apply the skeleton method. What is actually claimed — and how strong is it?","choices":["Report contributed decline — a strong causal claim","Report suggests (that the policy may have contributed) — the verb 'suggests' and the hedge 'may have' make it a weak, qualified claim, not a finding","Policy declined — the policy failed","Nothing is claimed; the sentence is meaningless"],"answer":1,"explain":"Skeleton: report suggests [X]. The engine is 'suggests' — not 'proves,' 'shows,' or 'demonstrates' — and the content is doubly hedged ('may have contributed'). The method reveals the fog's trick: an impressive sentence whose actual claim is near-zero commitment. This is the lesson's news-reading drill applied: find the verb, weigh the claim."}
],
"logic": [
{"q":"An argument is valid, its premises are all false, and its conclusion is true. What follows?","choices":["The argument is sound","The argument proves nothing — validity with false premises is unsound, and a true conclusion from it is luck, not knowledge","The argument is invalid","False premises always make the conclusion false"],"answer":1,"explain":"Run the two questions: (1) valid? Yes — structure guarantees the conclusion IF the premises held. (2) sound? No — the premises are false. Per lesson 1, only soundness gives knowledge, and per the 'lucky guess' rule, a true conclusion from an unfueled machine establishes nothing. You'd need true premises for the conclusion to be earned."},
{"q":"'If the dam holds, the town is safe. The town is not safe. Therefore the dam did not hold.' Name the form and judge it.","choices":["Affirming the consequent — invalid","Modus tollens — valid in form, though the premises could still be false","Denying the antecedent — invalid","Begging the question — circular"],"answer":1,"explain":"If P then Q; not-Q; therefore not-P — modus tollens, valid. The form is airtight: a held dam guaranteeing safety is incompatible with an unsafe town. But validity isn't soundness — maybe the town is unsafe for unrelated reasons and the guarantee was false. Form passes; premises still need checking."},
{"q":"Spot the fallacy: 'Either we cut the program or the department goes bankrupt.'","choices":["False dilemma — more options exist (trim, restructure, phased cuts, new revenue)","Slippery slope — no chain of events is given","Ad hominem — no person is attacked","Post hoc — no timing claim is made"],"answer":0,"explain":"False dilemma welds two extremes and deletes the middle. The lesson's test: ask what options were removed — here, partial cuts, restructuring, fundraising, merging programs. Note why the distractors fail: there's no predicted chain (slippery slope), no person attacked (ad hominem), no timing argument (post hoc). Naming requires matching the actual move."},
{"q":"Spot the fallacy: 'That study must be wrong — it was funded by the fossil-fuel industry.'","choices":["Genetic fallacy — judging the claim by its origin rather than its evidence","Strawman — the study isn't restated","Hasty generalization — no generalization is made","Tu quoque — no hypocrisy charge is made"],"answer":0,"explain":"Genetic fallacy: the claim is dismissed by its source, not engaged on its evidence. Funding is a reason for scrutiny — check methods, replication, data — not a refutation. The lesson's version: 'that idea came from them, so it's wrong.' Origin tells you where to look harder, never what you'll find."},
{"q":"Spot the fallacy: 'We should ban the book because dangerous books have no place on shelves.'","choices":["Begging the question — 'dangerous' smuggles the conclusion (it should be banned) into the premise","False dilemma — more than two options exist","Cherry-picking — no data is cited at all","Slippery slope — no chain is predicted"],"answer":0,"explain":"Begging the question: the premise ('dangerous books have no place') already assumes the book is the kind of thing that should be banned — which is exactly what's contested. The lesson's example has the same structure ('censor misinformation because false claims are dangerous and must be censored'): the conclusion rides inside the premise, so the argument goes in a circle."},
{"q":"A poll of 50 people from one neighborhood is cited as proof of a city-wide trend. Using the lesson's standards for induction, what is wrong?","choices":["Nothing — 50 is a large number","It fails sample size, variety, and method: 50 self-selected neighbors can't support a city-wide generalization — hasty generalization","It commits affirming the consequent","Polls are always deductive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Induction's strength depends on size, variety, and method (lesson 5) — one neighborhood fails all three: too few, too similar, non-random. That's hasty generalization wearing a poll's clothes. The black-swan point applies: one contrary neighborhood breaks the universal claim, and here we haven't even sampled it."},
{"q":"Your car won't start: lights are dim, there's a clicking sound. 'Dead battery' is your abduction. What makes this good abduction rather than guessing — and what could overturn it?","choices":["Nothing could overturn it — abduction is certain","It fits all the evidence and beats rivals (alternator, starter) for now — but it stays revisable: if a jump start fails, the verdict changes","It uses more data than deduction does","Guessing is fine as long as you're confident"],"answer":1,"explain":"Good abduction meets the lesson's three constraints: accounts for all evidence (dim lights + clicking both fit a dead battery), survives rivals (alternator/starter explain less neatly), and stays revisable. The jump-start test is revisability made concrete — new evidence re-ranks the explanations. That's the discipline of assent applied to a driveway."},
{"q":"Headline: 'Study proves coffee cures baldness.' Run the capstone's teardown. What are the first three moves?","choices":["Share it, cite it, celebrate it","State the actual claim in your own words; list the premises marked verified/unverified/false; name the argument's form and judge validity","Check who funded it and dismiss it if industry-funded","Count the fallacies and stop there"],"answer":1,"explain":"The capstone's order: (1) fair restatement — no strawmanning ('proves' and 'cures' are doing heavy lifting; pin them down); (2) premises audited individually — what did the study actually measure, on whom, with what controls?; (3) form and validity. Funding scrutiny (a genetic-fallacy risk if used as dismissal) and fallacy-counting come later, in service of the verdict: what does the evidence actually support?"},
{"q":"Spot the fallacy: 'My opponent wants to defund the police entirely' — when she actually proposed a 5% budget reallocation.","choices":["Strawman — restating the position in a weaker, more extreme form, then defeating the restatement","Ad hominem — attacking her character","Post hoc — confusing timing with cause","Begging the question — circular reasoning"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strawman: 'defund entirely' is a weaker, more extreme restatement of 'reallocate 5%.' Defeating the restatement ('defunding is crazy') scores points against a position she doesn't hold. The lesson's definition is exact — the fallacy is in the restatement step, before any defeating begins. Steelman first; then argue."},
{"q":"Distinguish: (A) 'You can't trust her climate plan; she flies private.' (B) 'You preach conservation but fly private.' Which is ad hominem, which tu quoque — and what do they share?","choices":["A is tu quoque, B is ad hominem; both are valid refutations","A is ad hominem (attacks the person to dismiss the argument), B is tu quoque (hypocrisy charge); both fail the same way — neither touches whether the plan itself works","Both are strawmen","A is sound, B is not"],"answer":1,"explain":"A aims at the person (untrustworthy, so dismiss the plan): ad hominem. B aims at the inconsistency (preaching vs. practice): tu quoque. The lesson's shared verdict: neither engages the argument's premises or structure — her travel habits, however damning as character evidence, don't change the climate math. Character charges never refute claims."}
],
"rhetoric": [
{"q":"Put Cicero's six parts in order.","choices":["Peroratio, exordium, narratio, confirmatio, refutatio, partitio","Exordium, narratio, partitio, confirmatio, refutatio, peroratio","Narratio, exordium, refutatio, partitio, peroratio, confirmatio","Confirmatio, refutatio, exordium, peroratio, narratio, partitio"],"answer":1,"explain":"Exordium (opening), narratio (facts), partitio (roadmap), confirmatio (your case), refutatio (objections answered), peroratio (close). The order is functional: win attention, agree on facts, show the map, build the case, defuse resistance, then close. Scrambling it — arguing before facts, closing before objections — breaks the psychology."},
{"q":"A speaker skips the narratio and launches straight into argument. What is lost?","choices":["Nothing — facts slow speeches down","The shared neutral ground: without agreed facts, the audience can't follow or trust the case built on them","The roadmap — but that's the partitio's job","The emotional appeal — facts kill pathos"],"answer":1,"explain":"The narratio's job is a brief, neutral account of what happened — no argument yet. It builds the common ground the confirmatio stands on. Skipping it means arguing from facts the audience hasn't granted: they spend the case wondering 'is that even what happened?' instead of weighing your reasoning. Argument before agreement on facts is building on sand."},
{"q":"Match each to its stasis: (i) 'Did the CEO know about the fraud?' (ii) 'Was it fraud or an accounting error?' (iii) 'Was she justified in staying silent?' (iv) 'Should she resign?'","choices":["(i) definition, (ii) conjecture, (iii) policy, (iv) quality","(i) conjecture, (ii) definition, (iii) quality, (iv) policy","(i) quality, (ii) policy, (iii) conjecture, (iv) definition","(i) policy, (ii) quality, (iii) definition, (iv) conjecture"],"answer":1,"explain":"(i) Did it happen? — conjecture. (ii) What do we call it? — definition. (iii) Was it justified? — quality. (iv) What should we do? — policy. The lesson's payoff: a board arguing (iv) while half the room is still on (i) is doomed — establish the stasis before the argument, or everyone answers a different question."},
{"q":"Which style level for a technical manual — and why?","choices":["Grand — manuals should inspire","Plain — its job is to teach, and the plain style's virtues (concrete, active, no throat-clearing) serve instruction","Middle — manuals should please","All three at once for variety"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plain teaches, middle pleases, grand moves. A manual's job is instruction — the reader needs to do the thing, not feel moved. The plain style's rules (concrete over abstract, active voice, cut throat-clearing) are usability rules. Grand style here would be pompous; middle would be distracting. Style must fit the job."},
{"q":"Identify the device: 'Ask not what your country can do for you — ask what you can do for your country.'","choices":["Anaphora — repetition at the start","Tricolon — three parallel phrases","Antithesis — 'not X but Y' in balanced contrast","No device — plain style"],"answer":2,"explain":"Antithesis: the sentence pivots on 'not X but Y' — country-for-you versus you-for-country — in balanced halves. The contrast does the work: two opposed ideas weighed in one breath. (It also opens with repeated 'ask,' a touch of anaphora, but the named, load-bearing device is the antithesis.) The lesson lists it among the few schemes that earn their keep."},
{"q":"A speaker opens with forty charts of data to a cold, skeptical audience. What goes wrong, in the lesson's terms?","choices":["Nothing — logos is the strongest appeal","Logos never gets its hearing: ethos (trust) and pathos (care) are the gatekeepers, and neither has been established","Charts are always manipulation","The audience needed the grand style instead"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's order — ethos, pathos, logos 'in that order' — means the argument is heard last, not first. A cold audience hasn't decided to trust the speaker or care about the subject, so the charts wash over them. The fix isn't fewer charts; it's earning the hearing first: costly honesty for ethos, stakes and particulars for pathos, then the data."},
{"q":"Which topic (topos) asks 'What produces it? What does it produce?'","choices":["Definition","Comparison","Cause and effect","Testimony"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cause and effect: trace what produces the thing and what the thing produces. The lesson's five topics are 'places to look' for arguments when inspiration fails — definition (what is it?), comparison (what's it like?), cause/effect, testimony (who saw it?), consequences (what follows?). Knowing which place you're searching keeps invention systematic instead of hopeful."},
{"q":"The lesson says to pause after important sentences because 'silence is punctuation.' What does the pause do for the audience?","choices":["It gives the speaker a rest","It marks the sentence as important and gives the point room to land — the audience needs processing time that adrenaline denies them","It shows the speaker memorized the speech","It fills time to reach three minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Punctuation tells readers where the weight is; pauses do that for listeners. A beat of silence after a key sentence signals 'that mattered' and lets it settle — rushing on buries it. Combined with speaking slower than feels natural, it counteracts adrenaline, which speeds the speaker while the audience still needs time to absorb."},
{"q":"'Did you persuade, or merely perform?' What is the capstone actually testing with this question?","choices":["Whether the speech was exactly three minutes","Lasting effect versus momentary impression — persuasion changes the listener's mind beyond the room; performance only impresses inside it","Whether you used enough rhetorical devices","Whether the audience applauded"],"answer":1,"explain":"The capstone's 24-hour test — 'what survives is what worked' — separates the two. Performance aims at the room's reaction now; persuasion aims at the listener's beliefs tomorrow. A speech can score perfectly on delivery and fail the capstone if nothing stuck. The course's final standard is outward: it worked only if it moved someone else, lastingly."},
{"q":"Two ads for the same charity: Ad A tells an emotional story AND publishes full financials. Ad B tells the same story, publishes nothing, and hides overhead costs. Apply the lesson's ethical line.","choices":["Both are manipulation — emotion is always suspect","A is persuasion (pathos in service of a true, checkable logos); B is manipulation (pathos substituting for logos — it would not survive the audience learning the full facts)","B is better — data distracts from feeling","The line between them is unknowable"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson's test is full-facts survival: does the emotional appeal hold up once the audience knows everything? A's pathos serves a logos the donor can verify — persuasion. B's pathos replaces the missing accountability — manipulation. Same story, same tears; the ethics turn entirely on whether the feeling is backed by checkable truth or used to hide its absence."}
]
});
